                    <span class="text-[10px] text-ink-1 normal-case font-normal">{label}</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-1 leading-relaxed">
                    {desc}
                  </div>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            <p>
              The December 2, 2027 deadline is the result of the Digital Omnibus agreement (preliminary agreement May 7, 2026), which extended the original August 2026 Annex III deadline. <a href="/blog/eu-ai-act-omnibus-collapse">The path to that agreement was not straightforward</a>, and the obligations themselves were not softened, only delayed.
            </p>
            <p>
              December 2027 sounds distant. It is not. Building compliant audit infrastructure, classifying systems, completing technical documentation, and registering in the EU database requires six to eighteen months of preparation for most enterprises. The organizations that start in 2027 will be building under enforcement pressure.
            </p>

            <!-- Section 3 -->
            <h2 id="annex-iii">Annex III: the 10 high-risk categories</h2>
            <p>
              Annex III of the EU AI Act lists the categories of AI systems classified as High-Risk. If your organization deploys AI agents in any of these areas, full compliance obligations apply from December 2, 2027. <a href="/guides/classify-ai-systems">Use our classification guide</a> to determine where your systems land.
            </p>

            <div class="not-prose my-5 border border-line-0">
              {[
                { n: "01", area: "Biometric identification", examples: "Real-time or post-hoc remote biometric identification. Emotion recognition in professional contexts." },
                { n: "02", area: "Critical infrastructure", examples: "Safety components in energy grids, water systems, transportation networks. AI agents managing operational decisions in these environments." },
                { n: "03", area: "Education & vocational training", examples: "AI determining access to education, evaluating learning outcomes, assessing students during exams." },
                { n: "04", area: "Employment", examples: "Recruitment and screening. CV filtering. Performance evaluation. Task allocation. Monitoring, promotion, and termination decisions. This is the category most enterprises underestimate." },
                { n: "05", area: "Essential services", examples: "Creditworthiness assessment for private individuals. Risk scoring for credit and insurance decisions. Life insurance and health insurance risk classification." },
                { n: "06", area: "Law enforcement", examples: "Predictive policing. Polygraph-equivalent systems. Evidence reliability assessment." },
                { n: "07", area: "Migration & border control", examples: "Risk assessment for visa and asylum applications. Identity verification at borders." },
                { n: "08", area: "Justice & democratic processes", examples: "AI assisting courts in fact-finding or applying law. Influencing elections." },
